th1-surjection-curation-001+math1-bijection-curation-001+math1-union-curation-001+math1-proper-curation-001+math1-complement-curation-001+math1-complement-render-001+math1-division-curation-001+math1-reciprocal-curation-001+math1-inequality-curation-001+math1-inequality-feedback-001+math1-maximum-curation-001+math1-minimum-curation-001+math1-boundedset-curation-001+math1-endpoint-curation-001+audit-groups-20261027+topup-math-i-20261028+topup-math-ii-20261028+topup-pt-20261028+topup-math-iii-20261028","concept_id":"Year-1/2b-Probability-Distributions/joint-probability-mass-function.html","concept":{"concept_id":"Year-1/2b-Probability-Distributions/joint-probability-mass-function.html","questions":[{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_joint_probability_mass_function_9e54e2a8_001","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For discrete $X$ and $Y$, what does the joint mass function $p_{X,Y}(x,y)$ give?","options":["$\\mathbb{P}(X=x)\\,\\mathbb{P}(Y=y)$","$\\mathbb{P}(X=x\\mid Y=y)$","$\\mathbb{P}(X=x,\\,Y=y)$","$\\mathbb{P}(X\\le x,\\,Y\\le y)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ca4edcd3c98a0cc51d9c1e92","time_limit":25},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_joint_probability_mass_function_9e54e2a8_002","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which condition must every joint mass function satisfy?","options":["Nonnegative values summing to $1$ over all pairs","$\\sum_xp_{X,Y}(x,y)=1$ for each fixed value $y$","$p_{X,Y}(x,y)\\le\\tfrac{1}{2}$ for every pair","The masses on the diagonal $x=y$ sum to $1$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"7186e5942de370da4f51a6cd","time_limit":25},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_joint_probability_mass_function_9e54e2a8_003","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For arbitrary discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$, which formula always computes $\\mathbb{P}(X<Y)$ from their joint mass function?","options":["$\\sum_{(x,y)\\colon\\,x\\le y}p_{X,Y}(x,y)$","$\\sum_{(x,y)\\colon\\,x>y}p_{X,Y}(x,y)$","$\\sum_{(x,y)\\colon\\,x<y}p_X(x)p_Y(y)$","$\\sum_{(x,y)\\colon\\,x<y}p_{X,Y}(x,y)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"a282cd6b67a6d28aea50829c","time_limit":25},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_joint_probability_mass_function_9e54e2a8_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $X$ take exactly two distinct values $a$ and $b$, each with positive probability, and let $Y=X$. How is the joint probability mass distributed?","options":["All four pairs carry mass, with $p_{X,Y}(x,y)=p_X(x)p_X(y)$.","Only $(a,a)$ and $(b,b)$ carry mass, respectively $p_X(a)$ and $p_X(b)$.","The off-diagonal pairs $(a,b)$ and $(b,a)$ carry masses $p_X(a)$ and $p_X(b)$.","Only $(a,a)$ and $(b,b)$ carry mass, each necessarily equal to $\\tfrac{1}{2}$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"4ad58bf5232b58664b18b2e4","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_joint_probability_mass_function_9e54e2a8_005","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Suppose $X,Y\\in\\{0,1\\}$. Which list of masses, in the order $(0,0),(0,1),(1,0),(1,1)$, defines a joint mass function?","options":["$0.4,\\ 0.3,\\ 0.2,\\ 0.2$","$0.5,\\ 0.5,\\ 0.5,\\ -0.5$","$0.25,\\ 0.25,\\ 0.25,\\ 0.15$","$0.6,\\ 0,\\ 0,\\ 0.4$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"280b92f7f288edb30af7d8b1","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_joint_probability_mass_function_9e54e2a8_006","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"A joint mass function has $p(0,0)=0.1$, $p(1,0)=0.2$, $p(2,0)=0.1$, $p(0,1)=0.2$, $p(1,1)=0.3$, $p(2,1)=0.1$, with $p(x,y)=\\mathbb{P}(X=x,Y=y)$. What is $\\mathbb{P}(X+Y=2)$?","options":["$0.3$","$0.4$","$0.1$","$0.5$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"538b8d8ca8320f4f4db24d02","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_joint_probability_mass_function_9e54e2a8_007","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Let $p_{X,Y}(x,y)=c(x+y)$ for $x,y\\in\\{0,1,2\\}$ and let it be zero elsewhere. Which constant $c$ makes this a joint mass function?","options":["$\\tfrac{1}{9}$","$\\tfrac{1}{36}$","$\\tfrac{1}{18}$","$\\tfrac{1}{3}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c7aa46bb85328759722a2d91","time_limit":25},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_joint_probability_mass_function_9e54e2a8_009","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"$X$ and $Y$ indicate heads on two independent fair coins, and $S=X+Y$. What is $p_{X,S}(1,1)$?","options":["$\\tfrac{1}{2}$","$\\tfrac{1}{4}$","$\\tfrac{1}{8}$","$\\tfrac{3}{4}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"6637aac28cad9567588ac568","time_limit":25},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_joint_probability_mass_function_9e54e2a8_011","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For arbitrary discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$, let $Z=X+Y$. Which formula always gives the mass function of $Z$?","options":["$p_Z(z)=\\sum_xp_X(x)\\,p_Y(z-x)$","$p_Z(z)=\\sum_xp_{X,Y}(x,z-x)$","$p_Z(z)=p_X(z)+p_Y(z)$","$p_Z(z)=p_{X,Y}(\\tfrac{z}{2},\\tfrac{z}{2})$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"a36f0162df9fa9a79675ba66","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_joint_probability_mass_function_9e54e2a8_012","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For discrete $X$ and $Y$, suppose $p_X(x)>0$ and $p_Y(y)>0$. Which statement about $p_{X,Y}(x,y)$ is valid without further assumptions?","options":["It must be positive.","It must equal $p_X(x)p_Y(y)$.","It may be $0$.","It must equal $p_X(x)+p_Y(y)$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"f75994e0da53c5042848e527","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_joint_probability_mass_function_9e54e2a8_013","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which bound always holds for a joint mass?","options":["$p_{X,Y}(x,y)\\le p_X(x)\\,p_Y(y)$","$p_{X,Y}(x,y)\\ge p_X(x)\\,p_Y(y)$","$p_{X,Y}(x,y)\\le\\tfrac{1}{2}$","$p_{X,Y}(x,y)\\le\\min(p_X(x),p_Y(y))$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"2540af79d4dd549cc52ea051","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_joint_probability_mass_function_9e54e2a8_014","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"A fair die is rolled, giving $X$. Conditional on each die result $X=x$, $x$ independent fair coins are tossed and $Y$ counts their heads. What is $p_{X,Y}(2,1)$?","options":["$\\tfrac{1}{12}$","$\\tfrac{1}{6}$","$\\tfrac{1}{2}$","$\\tfrac{1}{24}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"992479fe2581d95645190d36","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_joint_probability_mass_function_9e54e2a8_015","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Two independent fair six-sided dice show $X$ and $Y$. What is $\\mathbb{P}(|X-Y|\\le1)$?","options":["$\\tfrac{11}{36}$","$\\tfrac{5}{18}$","$\\tfrac{1}{6}$","$\\tfrac{4}{9}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"acc72cb93234777f5e908b7e","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_joint_probability_mass_function_9e54e2a8_016","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Two cards are drawn without replacement from four cards numbered $1,2,3,4$, choosing uniformly among the remaining cards at each draw. Let $X$ be the smaller number and $Y$ the larger. What is $p_{X,Y}(1,3)$?","options":["$\\tfrac{1}{12}$","$\\tfrac{1}{6}$","$\\tfrac{1}{16}$","$\\tfrac{1}{4}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"9195407eb42672c5d6b3e041","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_joint_probability_mass_function_9e54e2a8_017","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ and a fixed value $x$, what is $\\sum_y\\big[p_{X,Y}(x,y)-p_X(x)p_Y(y)\\big]$?","options":["$0$","$1$","$p_X(x)$","A positive number when $X$ and $Y$ are dependent"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"4c81dae1fce75e318589f6a3","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_joint_probability_mass_function_9e54e2a8_018","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"A fair coin $C$ is tossed. On heads, $X$ and $Y$ are independent, each taking $0$ and $1$ with probability $\\tfrac{1}{2}$. On tails, $X$ takes $0$ and $1$ with probability $\\tfrac{1}{2}$, and $Y=X$. Before observing $C$, are $X$ and $Y$ independent?","options":["Yes: both branches give each variable the same fair marginal distribution.","Yes: independence on the heads branch suffices for independence overall.","No: the unconditional mass $p_{X,Y}(1,1)=\\tfrac{3}{8}$ differs from the marginal product $\\tfrac{1}{4}$.","It depends: unconditional independence changes with the realised outcome of $C$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"6a8967a7c5ba0e8efa0e4145","time_limit":55},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_joint_probability_mass_function_9e54e2a8_019","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"Independent random variables $X$ and $Y$ take values in $\\{1,2,\\ldots\\}$, with $p_X(k)=p_Y(k)=2^{-k}$ for every integer $k\\ge1$. What is $\\mathbb{P}(X=Y)$?","options":["$\\tfrac{1}{4}$","$\\tfrac{1}{2}$","$\\tfrac{1}{3}$","$\\tfrac{2}{3}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ac2134b13b43a462028a56bb","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_joint_probability_mass_function_9e54e2a8_020","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"A discrete pair has joint mass $p_{X,Y}(x,y)=c(x+y)$ for $x,y\\in\\{1,2,3\\}$ and zero elsewhere, where $c$ is the normalising constant. Let $Z=XY$. What is $\\mathbb{P}(Z\\le3)$?","options":["$\\tfrac{4}{9}$","$\\tfrac{5}{9}$","$\\tfrac{2}{9}$","$\\tfrac{1}{18}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"e67f6d477cda34493c1588c2","time_limit":40}]}}